course: dictionary
lesson: 15138
title: Scarcely
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Scarcely is a small word with a big job. It means hardly, or almost not at all. «I could scarcely see the road in the fog.» Scarcely means you could only just see it, so the fog must have been very thick.
explain: Scarcely often comes before a word like any or ever. «There was scarcely any milk left in the jug.» Scarcely any means almost none, so the jug was nearly empty. «We scarcely ever go to the park now» means we almost never go.
explain: Scarcely can also show that one thing happened almost straight after another. «Scarcely had the match begun when the rain started.» The rain started almost as soon as the match began, so you can use the word when to follow scarcely.
fact: Scarcely means hardly, or almost not at all.
fact: «Scarcely any» means almost none, and «scarcely ever» means almost never.
fact: «Scarcely … when» means one thing happened almost straight after another.
fact: Scarcely can also mean surely not: «You can scarcely blame him» means you surely cannot blame him.
fact: Scarcely is a formal word, so you will often find it in stories and in books for older readers.
exercise: «I could scarcely believe my eyes when I saw the giant kite.» What does scarcely mean here? | Completely and fully | Again and again | Very happily | *Hardly, almost not at all
exercise: «There was scarcely any milk left in the jug.» What does scarcely any mean? | A lot of | Plenty, more than enough | Exactly the right amount | *Almost none
exercise: «Scarcely had the match begun when the rain started.» What does this tell you? | The match ended before the rain | *The rain started almost as soon as the match began | The rain started a whole day later | The match was cancelled before it began
test: «She had scarcely finished her homework when the bell rang for bed.» What does scarcely … when show in this sentence? | She never finished her homework | She finished after bedtime | *She finished almost just before the bell rang | She finished a week before the bell rang
test: «We scarcely ever go to the park now.» What does scarcely ever mean? | *Almost never | Always | Every day | Only on Sundays
exam: Which word could replace scarcely in «I can scarcely hear you» without changing the meaning? | Clearly | Loudly | *Hardly | Happily
